A||N||15||pherder||? on 440T4 shifter arm length||||5||
Z||000000||pherder||08-22-2001||08:28 PM||||To anyone running a 440T4/4T60 (<B>not electric</B>) transaxle, I am asking for a measurement of the distance from the center pivot point to the center of the pivot point where the shift cable attaches.<P>Thanks to Rockcrawl and others on the Forum, I have gotten almost <B>everything</B> together to put my 90 version of a 440T4 transaxle (out of a Grand Prix) in my 86 SE.<P>I got the axles in from CV Unlimited.  The mounting brackets off a 6000, the engine to transaxle bracket from a 2.8 (w/AOD), the proper neutral safety switch and the electrical plug for the TCC.  <P>The guys at the local Salvage yard have been a great help with allowing me to get parts from many different cars to make this work.<P>The only thing I haven't figured out yet is the shifter arm length but can given some trial and error but if someone can help out here with this info, I can go ahead and get this swap started this weekend.<P>TIA!<P>Paul||||reg||5
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||08-22-2001||11:48 PM||||pherder- It's rather late now, but I'll check the forum in the morning and go out and check mine- (tranny) and let you know!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">     <P>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||08-23-2001||01:30 PM||||I'm assuming that the piece noted here is what you're looking for. I measure it to be 1 5/8 inch from center to center. I hope this helps!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/shifter1r.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">      <P>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett<P>Edit: Corrected Photo<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||MinnGreenGT||08-23-2001||01:33 PM||||Figured I'd also add that a slightly different size piece may change the shift pattern.  I can't quite get my 4 spd down into 1st... If I adjust it farther, I can't get it into Park- So I kept park and went with 2nd.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">     <P>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||08-23-2001||02:14 PM||||MinnGreenGT,<P>Thanks for the measurment!<P>One more, if you please ... the distance from the piviot point where the cable attaches to the cable mount ... i.e. how long is the cable from where it?<P>As in this picture ...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/440t4_shifter_cable_length.jpg"> <P>My T125 is ~ 5 3/4" from inside of the bracket to the center of the cable piviot.<P>TIA!!!<P>Paul||||reg||14
Z||000005||rockcrawl||08-23-2001||04:30 PM||||Paul, the arm should be 1.725" center to center to be able to hit all gears. The shifter <B>does not</B> need to be modified, it already has the extra detent for the "new" low gear. Hop in a stock Fiero and pop it in low gear, there is still another unused spot on the shifter below that. With the arm at 1.725" all the gears fall into a detent on the shifter just the way they should. The orange shift indicator lines up perfectly in every gear down to where it says 1 (actually in 2nd with the new trans), but when you shift into the new low gear the indicator disappears off the end.<P>When in park, the mount bracket should be about 6.25" from the center of the ball stud on the arm. If you don't already have one, get one of the adjustable cables so you don't have to get this perfect. just make the bracket close and then fine tune it by adjusting the cable in or out. I haven't figured out which Fieros have the adjustable cable, but I think it's a year change thing. Or you could just slot the holes in the bracket and make adjutments that way.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||08-23-2001||04:44 PM||||Ouch... just got back from lunch- hot engine.  I measure that length (call it "B") to be 5.75 (5 3/4) inches from the inside edge of the bracket to the center of the link/joint.  Let me know if you need any other measures- glad to help!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000007||MinnGreenGT||08-23-2001||04:57 PM||||rockcrawl... none of the parts were modified on my tranny or shifter equip. BUT I still can't quite grab Park and 1st easily.  I can pull my shifter into the hidden notch, but it never locks into 1st (it might hold there, but it doesn't lock).  This isn't mathmatical- I spent quite some time adjusting it to try to get it to grab all the positions EASILY.  As to the difference in measured distance for "B" -I just measured mine... 5.75"  and the 1/10th of an inch difference in our other measurement... I did it leaning over my car wearing work clothes with a tape meausre- I think 1/10th of an inch is still within tolerance.<P>{EDIT} Oh yeah, and all my indicators do line up correctly too. (none=1, 1=2, 2=D, D=OD)<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||pherder||08-23-2001||05:33 PM||||First, I would like to thank you both for your information.  Why reinvent the wheel?<P>Second, MinnGreenGT and I both start out with what stock is for the measurement between the bracket (inside) to the cable mounting piviot ... 5.75".  That is where the stock cable is when the stock transaxle is in Park.  I don't know if there is a diffenence in the cables between years of the Fiero but I will make my bracket adjustable to allow for adjustment.<P>I find the two measurements for the length of the shift arm interesting.  I would have thought that MinnGreenGT's arm was longer and that is why his wouldn't engage Park or 1st as described.  But since his is <B>shorter!</B>, I am totally confused!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  A shorter arm would move <B>LESS</B> in it's travel between Park and 1st than a longer arm and Park and 1st <B>should</B> be easiler to engage.<P>Guess I will see when I get mine in the car!<P>Again, thanks!<P>Paul<P>||||reg||7
Z||000009||KissMySSFiero||08-23-2001||07:31 PM||||kinda off subject,  but what kinda rpm's do you run with the OD?  with the 2.8 is there any performance gain as far as acceleration?  TIA<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000010||pherder||08-23-2001||08:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B>kinda off subject,  but what kinda rpm's do you run with the OD?  with the 2.8 is there any performance gain as far as acceleration?  TIA<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 440T4/4T60 has the same first three ratios as the T125.  It has an overdrive to bring down the rpms while cruising.<P>So other than the FDRs, it is the same.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000011||pherder||08-24-2001||03:09 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>I'm assuming that the piece noted here is what you're looking for. I measure it to be 1 5/8 inch from center to center. I hope this helps!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/shifter1r.jpg">    <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>MinnGreenGT, <P>I might have a idea on why you are having a problem with getting 1st but first a question.<P>In the above picture, is the transaxle in park?<P>If so, the angle of the cable bracket is too steep.  The shifter arm goes from park clockwise to 1st and the throw is almost 90*.<P>When you are trying to select 1st, the cable is trying to push off the shifter arm instead of pushing the arm to the 1st gear.<P>What I will be trying to do is I have put the transaxle in Park and marked the location.  Then I have put it in 1st and marked that.  From those two points, I have drawn a straight line and my bracket will be facing that line.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/440T4_shifter_bracket_mod.jpg">  <P>The red line is where 1st would be, the green line is the straight line between 1st and park, and the blue rectangle is where the bracket would be to have the straightest throw for the shifter's full movement using the positions of the items in your picture (i.e. NOT to scale!)<P>Also on mine, the shifter arm is in Park when it is overtop the one mounting bolt head for the neutral safety switch so my bracket would be down on the lower end of your picture.<P>BTW, if your picture shows you in any other position other than Park, nevermind!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>But you should check out the straight line to your bracket anyway.<P>Paul<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 08-24-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000012||MinnGreenGT||08-24-2001||04:59 PM||||Paul-<P>Hmmmm.... Yes, the car was fully locked in the park position when I shot the photo (leaning over the drivers side rear sailpanel). I'll have to take a look at the setup vs. your statements (I'm stuck at work for awhile longer) later.  Thanks for the suggestion.  BTW: Do you know how to get the tranny to lock up after it's shifted into 4th?  I get my best mileage with the Cruise set at 95-100mph or about 3000rpm.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000013||pherder||08-25-2001||10:09 AM||||Rob,<P>One other idea ... you used the stock 440T4/4T60 shifter arm (cut down) ... If you used the stock T125 Fiero arm, it would put the over the mounting bolt on the left side and you could adjust your cable bracket for a straighter shot.<P>Just a thought ... I noticed it when I found my stock 440T4/4T60 arm and compared it to the stock Fiero's T125 arm and remembered your picture showing the same angle.<P>Paul||||reg||3
Z||000014||MinnGreenGT||08-26-2001||09:41 PM||||Paul-<P>Just wondering if you knew how to handle the TCC lockup setup for the 440?  My tranny shifts into 4th fine, but won't lockup 'cause I haven't adjusted the wiring at the front TCC plug. <P>Also I was discussing this topic with my <BR>Dad (mechanic) tonight- he suggested getting an adjustable cable instead of the stock ('87) lenght cable... said it would have made adjustments MUCH easier.  <BR>Also wondered if i had mentioned to anyone that there was a hole cut by a torch in my frame... he did it to get a socket onto a specific bolt on the new tranny!  I told him I'd just tell everyone it was  a weight saving effort  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000015||pherder||08-26-2001||09:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>Paul-<P>Just wondering if you knew how to handle the TCC lockup setup for the 440?  My tranny shifts into 4th fine, but won't lockup 'cause I haven't adjusted the wiring at the front TCC plug. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>IAW, another web page I have seen on the 440T4/4T60 swap, the A and D connections for the TCC are the same between the two transaxles.  The difference inside is that the T125 has a pressure switch for 3rd gear which means that you have to be in third for the TCC to lock.  IAW the same web page, he has hooked up the Fiero's A and D connection to the 440T4/4T60 transaxle and has lock up of the TCC in both 3rd and 4th and has normal shifting the rest of the time.<P>That is what I am going to do.  I have the correct plug (they are "keyed" differently) and will wire it inplace of the stock Fiero's plug.<P>Be advised ... A and D must be reconnected exactly the same since there is a diode in the circuit and it has to have the current going thru it the same way to work!<P>BTW, got my bracket and shifter arm welded up today by a friend who has a $3000 TIG setup as a home hobbyest!!  I traded a Mazda Protege rear trunk latch (for his 32 street rod he is building) for the welding!  I think I got the better of the deal since I got the latch for ~ $5 at a salvage yard!<P>Tomorrow I will start tearing down my 86SE and get it ready for the engine/transaxle swap!<P>Paul||||reg||1
